<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Parliament of Scotland</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th century–<a href="/years/1707">1707</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Scottish Parliament (pre-1707), Estates of Scotland</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Parliament of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> was the national legislature of the Kingdom of Scotland from the thirteenth century until 1707. It met as a single chamber of three estates, clergy, nobility and burgh commissioners, much of whose business was prepared by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lords-of-the-articles">Lords of the Articles</a>. It grew more assertive after 1689 and in 1707 ratified the Treaty of Union, which ended it; a new Scottish Parliament was opened in 1999.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/parliament-of-scotland.jpg" alt="Parliament of Scotland" width="320" height="310" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Royal_Coat_of_Arms_of_the_Kingdom_of_Scotland.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
